{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192609": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Biology Department Location: Lexington, VA, USA The Department of Biology at Washington and Lee University invites applications for one tenure-track Assistant Professor position for the 2027-2028 academic year, beginning July 1, 2027.  We seek an integrative biologist to support the biology program.  Disciplinary and research interests are open, but we prefer candidates working across multiple levels (molecular, population, ecosystem) and across traditional disciplines.  This person will also support our growing Data Science Minor with an average of two courses per year.  We favor an excellent educator with genuine interests in teaching and pursuing research with undergraduate students. Washington and Lee University promotes a dynamic and welcoming environment that allows students and employees of multiple backgrounds and perspectives to learn, work, and thrive together. Successful candidates will contribute to that environment and exhibit potential for excellence in teaching and for vigorous scholarship. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. The school calendar consists of two 12-week terms, one in the fall and one in the winter, and a 4-week spring term. The teaching load is 5 courses per year; there is no summer teaching. Support for research includes laboratory start-up funding, conference funding, summer research funding, and pre-tenure leave, as well as opportunities to present to faculty cohorts and programs on campus; teaching support is available through university-wide workshops, a faculty mentoring program, and the Harte Center for Teaching and Learning . Washington and Lee University is a highly selective, independent, co-educational, liberal arts college of approximately 1850 undergraduate and 400 law students and located in Lexington, VA, three hours southwest of Washington, DC. W&L is consistently ranked among the top 12 national liberal arts colleges. Established in 1749, it is the ninth oldest institution of higher learning in the nation. The expected salary for this position is between $94,000 and $105,000 per year. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding, a strong culture of mentorship, and a pre-tenure leave program. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . Preferred candidates will hold a Ph.D. in Biology or adjacent fields, show promise in research, and demonstrate interest and experience in teaching. Review of applications will begin October 5, 2026. Candidates should submit: A letter of application summarizing teaching interests and experience, scholarly pursuits, and demonstrating the ability to work with students with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences a CV names and contact details of three references who are willing and able to write confidential letters of recommendation (we will solicit the letters when and as needed) All materials should be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/192609 . Our search committee will conduct initial interviews virtually. After application review, finalists will be invited to campus. For more information, please contact Paul Cabe at cabep@wlu.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192513": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Economics (Macroeconomics)",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Economics Location: Lexington, VA The Department of Economics in the Williams School of Commerce, Economics, and Politics at Washington and Lee University invites applications from candidates with teaching and research interests in macroeconomics for a tenure-track position at the rank of assistant professor of Economics. The appointment will begin July 1, 2027. The successful candidate will teach the department’s required macroeconomics courses (Principles of Macroeconomics and Macroeconomic Theory) and develop electives that build on existing departmental strengths while expanding opportunities for students. Washington and Lee University is a highly selective, private, liberal arts college, located in Lexington, VA (population 7,000). W&L’s Economics department offers an undergraduate major in Economics and supports interdisciplinary minors in Africana Studies; Data Science; Education Studies; Environmental Studies; Law, Justice & Society; Middle East & South Asian Studies; Poverty & Human Capability; and Women, Gender & Sexuality Studies. This position requires a strong commitment to excellence in teaching within an undergraduate liberal arts setting. Candidates should demonstrate the capacity to publish high-quality scholarly research. The university supports faculty research through a generous five-year sabbatical cycle, a pre-tenure research leave program, opportunities for summer salary support, travel stipends, and paid undergraduate research assistants. The department also hosts a seminar series that meets regularly throughout the academic year and features internal and external speakers. The school calendar consists of two 13-week terms, one in the fall and one in the winter, along with our unique 4-week spring term. The regular teaching load is five courses per year. New hires teach a 2-2-0 load in their first year to provide an opportunity to fully participate in W&L’s New Faculty Cohort, a comprehensive, year-long faculty development program. Washington and Lee University promotes a dynamic and welcoming environment that allows students and employees of multiple backgrounds and perspectives to learn, work, and thrive together. Successful candidates will contribute to that environment and exhibit potential for excellence in teaching and for rigorous scholarship. We welcome applications from candidates who would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. The expected salary for this position is between $112,000- $118,000. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding, a strong culture of mentorship, and a pre-tenure leave program. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . A Ph.D. in Economics is required by the time of appointment. Review of applications will begin November 13, 2026. Candidates should submit the following materials through the Interfolio portal at https://apply.interfolio.com/192513 : Letter of Application summarizing your teaching interests and experience, your interest in a liberal arts environment, your scholarly contributions or plans, and your ability to work with students with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences CV Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness including a teaching statement, representative syllabi (if applicable), list of courses taught, and course evaluations (please note: “Selected comments” are not considered a substitute for full course evaluations) Three confidential letters of recommendation Job Market Paper If you have questions about the position, please contact Katie Shester, chair of the search committee, at shesterk@wlu.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189475": {
      "title": "The Donald W. Reynolds Chair in Business Journalism",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Journalism & Mass Communications Department Location: Lexington, VA Washington and Lee University’s Department of Journalism and Mass Communications seeks an experienced journalist of national reputation to hold The Donald W. Reynolds Chair in Business Journalism, beginning in July 2027. The Reynolds Chair runs a signature program, the highly successful business journalism sequence, launched in 2002 to give students specialized education in reporting with an emphasis on covering business. W&L’s Department of Journalism and Mass Communications is the nation’s only accredited journalism program in a highly competitive liberal arts university. We seek a colleague who can adapt and grow the business journalism program to meet the challenges of new technology and a changing business climate. The business journalism program combines two of Washington and Lee’s historic strengths—its accredited journalism department and its Williams School of Commerce, Economics and Politics. Duties include teaching four courses each school year – including classes about reporting on financial news, local communities and the economy – along with sharing in teaching duties for the department’s student-run news program, The Rockbridge Report . See video and text stories from The Rockbridge Report here: https://rockbridgereport.academic.wlu.edu/ The Reynolds Chair also coordinates visits of high-profile professionals in business media to campus and oversees an internship program that provides students with experience at business media outlets. The expected salary for this position is between $140,000 - $185,000. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. The Chair is an endowed position at the rank of professor with tenure. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding and a strong culture of mentorship. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit https://www.wlu.edu/employment-opportunities . Washington and Lee University promotes a dynamic and welcoming environment that allows students and employees of multiple backgrounds and perspectives to learn, work, and thrive together. Successful candidates will contribute to that environment and exhibit potential for excellence in teaching and for rigorous scholarship. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. Candidates must have extensive business journalism experience and should have a graduate degree. Some experience in media management and teaching is also preferred. Candidates also must have robust experience and accomplishments that support their hiring as a full professor with tenure. To be considered, applicants should submit the following materials through the Interfolio portal at https://apply.interfolio.com/189475: CV Cover letter addressing your professional and academic background and your interests in business journalism and teaching. Names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin on Oct. 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For further information, contact Professor Eric Deggans at edeggans@wlu.edu, 120 Reid Hall, Department of Journalism and Mass Communications, Washington and Lee University, Lexington, Virginia 24450."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189553": {
      "title": "J. Ramsey Martin Professorship in Philosophy",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Philosophy Department Location: Lexington, VA The Department of Philosophy at Washington and Lee University invites applications for the inaugural J. Ramsey Martin Professorship in Philosophy. Established through the generosity of the William H. Miller III Foundation, the J. Ramsey Martin Professorship honors the legacy of Joseph Ramsey Martin Jr., who taught philosophy at Washington and Lee from 1968 to 1996 and inspired generations of students through his scholarship, teaching, and mentorship. The position will begin July 1, 2027. This position carries a 5-course teaching load distributed over Fall and Winter terms (12 weeks each), and the university’s signature 4-week Spring term. Washington and Lee is a highly selective, nationally ranked liberal arts university committed to excellence in teaching and scholarship. The Philosophy Department offers a vibrant curriculum that encourages rigorous engagement with philosophical traditions while fostering thoughtful inquiry into contemporary questions. The successful candidate will play a leading role in advancing the department’s contributions to the university’s core curriculum and broader liberal arts mission. Washington and Lee University promotes a dynamic and welcoming environment that allows students and employees of multiple backgrounds and perspectives to learn, work, and thrive together. Successful candidates will contribute to that environment and exhibit excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. The expected salary for this position is between $100,000 - $170,000. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, rank, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding, a strong culture of mentorship, and a pre-tenure leave program. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Philosophy and qualify for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor with tenure. They must have as an area of specialization ethics or political philosophy, especially historical approaches as applied to contemporary issues. Area of competence is open. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active program of scholarship, teach undergraduate courses at all levels, contribute to departmental and university service, and engage actively in the intellectual life of the campus. Application materials should include a letter of application, curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, statement of scholarly agenda, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applications will be accepted through Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/189553 . For more information about the Department of Philosophy and Washington and Lee University, please visit www.wlu.edu . For inquiries contact Prof. Nathaniel Goldberg at goldbergn@wlu.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191114": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Physics and Engineering Department Location: Lexington, VA The Department of Physics and Engineering at Washington and Lee University invites applications for a tenure-track position in engineering at the rank of Assistant Professor.  This new position will begin in July 2027 and was created to support the pursuit of ABET accreditation for our long-standing and thriving engineering program.  Candidates should be excited to teach across the engineering curriculum, including first-year introductory courses, required sophomore and junior engineering courses and labs, and upper-level topical electives within the area of expertise.  Candidates should be prepared to mentor undergraduates in a productive research program; engineering-related area of research is open.  Startup funds, laboratory space, and summer research support are available. To achieve our mission as a liberal arts university, we strive to foster a welcoming campus community that recognizes the value of all persons. Our department is committed to preparing our students for engaged citizenship by creating a positive learning and working environment for all through open and substantive dialogue. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. The expected salary for this position is between $94,000 and $105,000. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding, a strong culture of mentorship, and a pre-tenure leave program. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . A Ph.D. in a field of engineering is required by the time of appointment.  Undergraduate teaching or professional engineering experience is highly desirable. Review of applicants will begin October 15, 2026.  Applicants should submit the following to Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/191114 : A letter of application Current CV Statement of teaching philosophy Description of research plans including how undergraduates will be involved Three confidential letters of recommendation."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192170": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Physics",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Physics and Engineering Department Location: Lexington, VA The Department of Physics and Engineering at Washington and Lee University invites applications for a tenure-track position in physics at the rank of Assistant Professor. This new position will begin in July 2027. Candidates should be excited to teach across the physics curriculum, including first-year introductory courses, required upper division physics courses and labs, and topical electives within their area of expertise. Candidates should be prepared to mentor undergraduates in a productive research program; the specific physics-related area of research is open.  Startup funds, laboratory space, and summer research support are available. To achieve our mission as a liberal arts university, we strive to foster a welcoming campus community that recognizes the value of all persons. Our department is committed to preparing our students for engaged citizenship by creating a positive learning and working environment for all through open and substantive dialogue. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding, a strong culture of mentorship, and a pre-tenure leave program. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . The expected salary for this position is between $94,000 and $105,000 per year. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. A Ph.D. in physics or a related field is required by the time of appointment.  Undergraduate teaching experience is highly desirable. Review of applicants will begin October 21, 2026.  Applicants should submit the following to Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/192170 : A letter of application outlining current and future scholarship, summarizing teaching interests and experience, and demonstrating the ability to work with students with a broad range of backgrounds and experiences Current CV Brief statement of teaching philosophy Brief description of research plans, including how undergraduates will be involved Three confidential letters of recommendation"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193723": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice in Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Physics and Engineering Department Location: Lexington, VA The Department of Physics and Engineering at Washington and Lee University invites applications for a professor of practice in engineering.  This new non-tenure track, renewable position will begin in July 2027 and was created to support the pursuit of ABET accreditation for our long-standing and thriving engineering program.  Candidates should be prepared to teach the engineering design process and project management fundamentals across the undergraduate curriculum from first-year introductory design classes to the year-long capstone design sequence, manage the placement of rising seniors onto capstone projects, and lead the development of a consistent program-wide approach to teaching the engineering design process.  Candidates should be motivated to pursue and maintain industry and community-based partnerships that result in capstone projects, community-based class projects, internships, and externships. To achieve our mission as a liberal arts university, we strive to foster a welcoming campus community that recognizes the value of all persons. Our department is committed to preparing our students for engaged citizenship by creating a positive learning and working environment for all through open and substantive dialogue. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. The expected salary for this position is between $110,000 and $120,000. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding, a strong culture of mentorship, and a pre-tenure leave program. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . A master's degree in engineering or related field or reasonable equivalent and ten years of experience in managing engineering design within industry are required.  A current professional license in engineering is strongly preferred.  Undergraduate teaching or mentorship experience is highly desirable. Review of applicants will begin November 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled.  Applicants should submit the following to Interfolio https://apply.interfolio.com/193723 : A letter of application Current CV Statement of teaching philosophy Description of experience in managing design within industry and how that experience will translate into teaching and mentoring undergraduate students Contact information for three confidential letters of recommendation"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189114": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Cognitive and Behavioral Science",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: Cognitive and Behavioral Science Location: Lexington, VA The Cognitive and Behavioral Science (CBSC) Department at Washington and Lee University ( https://my.wlu.edu/cognitive-and-behavioral-science ) seeks to fill a tenure track Assistant Professor position, beginning July 1, 2027. This position carries a 5-course teaching load distributed over Fall and Winter terms (12 weeks each), and the university’s signature 4-week Spring term. Teaching responsibilities include one of our introductory courses (Developmental, Social, or Cognitive Psychology), our Statistics course, courses in their area of expertise, and supervision of student research. To achieve our mission as a liberal arts college, we strive to foster a welcoming campus community, which recognizes the value of all persons. Our department is committed to preparing our students for engaged citizenship by creating a positive learning and working environment for all through open and substantive dialogue. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and Strategic Plan. The expected salary for this position is between $94,000 - $105,000. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, tenure status, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through research funding, a strong culture of mentorship, and a pre-tenure leave program. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . Competitive applicants will hold a Ph.D. in Psychology or a closely allied field and demonstrate a commitment to in-person teaching and engagement of undergraduates in research. The specific area of research expertise is open, but preference will be given to candidates who expand the disciplinary scope of our department’s faculty. We welcome candidates interested in joining a community-oriented department that values teaching innovation in inclusive spaces and mentoring students in their intellectual, personal, and professional development. Review of applications begins October 1, 2026 . Applicants should submit these materials to Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/189114 : 1) cover letter; 2) current CV; 3) three confidential letters of recommendation; 4) representative reprints; 5) statement of teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching effectiveness; and 6) description of research plans, including how undergraduates will be involved."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191078": {
      "title": "Research and Instruction Librarian for the Social Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Washington and Lee University: University Library Location: Lexington, VA Description Washington and Lee University Library welcomes applications from collaborative, user-focused, and inclusive early career librarians to join the Research Services team as Research and Instruction Librarian for the Social Sciences. Reporting to the Head of Research Services, the Research and Instruction Librarian for the Social Sciences will: serve as the primary liaison to disciplines in or related to the social sciences; contribute to general reference and instruction, collection management, and outreach activities in collaboration with the Research Services team; and participate in service and scholarship activities as a member of the University Library and College faculties. The Research and Instruction Librarian for the Social Sciences will be a 12-month, non-tenure track faculty member at the Assistant Professor rank. The appointment begins on or after January 1, 2027. About the University and the Library Washington and Lee University is a nationally ranked, private liberal arts institution located in Lexington, Virginia. W&L integrates the rigorous inquiry and critical thinking of a liberal arts curriculum with nationally accredited undergraduate programs in business and journalism, and a graduate School of Law. Our academic units are strengthened by meaningful collaborations, all in the service of the university’s mission. With an undergraduate enrollment of approximately 1,860, our small size enables students to find mentors and develop personal relationships across campus. W&L is committed to institutional citizenship. We regard liberal arts education as an important public good, of value not only to our students and alumni, but also to the larger communities in which we live. Washington and Lee University promotes a dynamic and welcoming environment that allows students and employees of multiple backgrounds and perspectives to learn, work, and thrive together. Successful candidates will contribute to that environment and exhibit potential for excellence in teaching and for rigorous scholarship. We welcome applications from candidates that would contribute to the University’s mission and strategic plan. The University Library serves W&L’s central mission of teaching, learning, and research in the liberal arts through close engagement with undergraduate and campus communities. We steward approximately 1.6M physical and digital items in 100,000+ square feet of public space with a total faculty/staff of 23. The University Library is the home of W&L's Digital Culture and Information (DCI) minor program. Library faculty serve as initial and minor program advisors for undergraduates, may teach courses in DCI and other disciplines, and participate in faculty governance, service, and scholarship opportunities. Essential and Related Functions Serves as the library’s primary liaison to academic departments in or related to the social sciences for the purposes of supporting disciplinary teaching and research and facilitating communication between the library and departments. This may include information literacy instruction, subject-based reference and consultative services, and departmental and individual outreach. As a member of the Research Services team, provides general outreach and expert research advice to students, faculty, staff, administrators, and community visitors. Collaborates with colleagues across the library to recommend selection and deselection of research and teaching resources for the library’s collection. Serves as a faculty member of the College. Participates in teaching activities of the library, which may include advising students and/or teaching credit bearing courses, among other opportunities. Engages in pertinent ongoing service, research, scholarship, and/or creative activity as defined by the library’s Departmental Faculty Development Document. Participates in professional development activities as appropriate to meet personal and departmental goals and objectives. Performs other duties as assigned by the University Librarian or Head of Research Services. Compensation The expected salary for this position is between $68,000 - $75,000. Offers are based on factors such as relevant education, experience, internal equity, and market and organizational considerations. Washington and Lee University offers a generous and comprehensive benefits package designed to support the well-being of employees and their families, as well as substantial support for faculty development through professional development funding and a strong culture of mentorship. To learn more about working at W&L, employee benefits, and life in the Shenandoah Valley, visit jobs.wlu.edu . ALA-accredited MLS or equivalent relevant terminal degree. Holding a terminal degree in a relevant field is a requirement of faculty status. Any combination of experience and coursework in the fundamentals of subject-based research support, such as information literacy, reference, instruction, and collection management. This may include pre-degree experience. Demonstrated interest in one or more of the following social science fields taught at Washington and Lee: sociology, anthropology, journalism, mass communication, education, or women and gender studies. Review of applications will begin September 14, 2026, and continue until position is filled. Candidates should submit: A letter of application describing interest in the position and addressing the qualifications. A complete professional CV. Names and contact information for three professional or academic references will be requested from finalists. All materials should be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/191078 . University Librarian K.T. Vaughan is eager to speak with potential candidates who have questions before applying. Please contact her at kvaughan@wlu.edu."
    }
  },
